<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  hockey-edge snapshot job — Windows Task Scheduler definition.

  Ready to register as-is (2026-09-02): the paths below are absolute and
  point at this checkout. A scheduled task inherits neither a shell's working
  directory nor its PATH, so <Command> must be the venv interpreter itself
  (NOT "python"), and every path here is spelled out in full.

    repo root  : C:\Users\Nikla_000\Documents\local-repo\hockey-edge
    interpreter: C:\Users\Nikla_000\Documents\local-repo\hockey-edge\.venv\Scripts\pythonw.exe

  pythonw.exe, not python.exe (changed 2026-09-03). python.exe allocates a
  console, so every 15-minute tick popped a window that stole keyboard focus
  from whatever was in front of it. <Hidden> does not help: for an Exec action
  it governs whether the task is listed in the Task Scheduler UI, not whether
  the process gets a console — the interpreter choice is the only thing that
  decides that. The cost is that pythonw runs with no console at all, so
  sys.stdout and sys.stderr are None and logs/snapshot_job.log is the sole
  record of a run. job.py is written for that: its console handler is
  installed only when sys.stderr exists, its log file is opened as UTF-8, and
  main() logs any otherwise-unhandled exception before exiting non-zero.

  On paths: the job's data/log locations are NOT cwd-relative — job.py,
  storage.py and raw_cache.py all anchor to Path(__file__).resolve().
  parents[3], i.e. the repo root as derived from the module's own location.
  Verified 2026-09-02 by running the job from C:\ and watching it find this
  repo's data/snapshots.db and logs/ correctly. <WorkingDirectory> is still
  set to the repo root, because job.py calls python-dotenv's load_dotenv(),
  whose .env discovery does depend on where the process starts.

  Trigger window: every 15 minutes, 11:00–23:00 local, daily.

  NOTE ON THE WINDOW: 11:00, not 14:00. Liiga's regular season is mostly
  17:00/18:30 starts, but not entirely — season 2027 has 15 RUNKOSARJA games
  starting before 17:00 local, including one at 12:00 (2026-10-07) and two at
  14:00 (2026-11-21, 2027-03-20). The binding constraint is not the T-24h
  window but the CLOSING one (T-25min), which is where a confirmed lineup
  actually appears (see docs/SNAPSHOT_FINDINGS.md's T-30 finding). With a
  14:00 start those three games' closing windows come due before the first
  tick, and storage.get_due_windows only returns a window while
  start_utc > now — so they would be recorded MISSED, permanently, rather
  than captured. 11:00 covers every scheduled 2026-27 fixture with margin and
  still never ticks overnight. Re-check this if a future season schedules
  anything earlier than ~11:30 local.

  WakeToRun=true (changed 2026-09-02, was false). The earlier false was a
  preseason choice: leave the desktop asleep and let the missed-tick recovery
  path get exercised for real, back when a missed August friendly cost
  nothing. That trade-off inverted once the season started — relying on the
  machine happening to be awake at T-25min makes the job close to useless in
  practice, and a missed capture is unrecoverable.

  Two things this depends on, both outside this file: the power plan must
  permit wake timers ("Allow wake timers" = Enabled), or WakeToRun silently
  does nothing; and a wake timer only wakes a machine that is ASLEEP, never
  one hibernated or shut down. Also be aware this asks Windows to wake for
  every 15-minute repetition inside the 11:00-23:00 window, not just for
  games — in practice the machine will stay up most of the day.

  StartWhenAvailable=true is still set and still matters: it covers the case
  where the machine was off/hibernated through a tick. The job's own
  due/missed logic (storage.get_due_windows / mark_missed_windows) remains
  what makes a late tick correct — never a naive "is now ≈ the window time"
  check. See job.py's docstring.

  REGISTRATION — what actually worked (PowerShell 7, confirmed 2026-09-02):

    $xml = Get-Content -Raw "docs\snapshot_job_task_scheduler.xml"
    $xml = $xml -replace '<\?xml[^>]*\?>', ''
    Register-ScheduledTask -TaskName "hockey-edge snapshot job" -Xml $xml

  The middle line is required, not cosmetic. Register-ScheduledTask takes the
  XML as a *string*, which PowerShell holds as UTF-16 in memory, so a document
  declaring any encoding at all is a contradiction the parser rejects
  ("Cannot use a prefix with an empty namespace" / malformed-XML style errors).
  Stripping the whole declaration sidesteps it. Note this is independent of
  the file's own encoding: the declaration was previously (and wrongly) UTF-16
  while the bytes were UTF-8, which breaks `schtasks /XML` — that is now fixed
  to UTF-8, but the PowerShell path still needs the declaration stripped.

  Alternatives, if the above is unavailable: `schtasks /Create /TN
  "hockey-edge snapshot job" /XML "<abs path to this file>"`, or Task
  Scheduler -> Action pane -> "Import Task...".

  Task Scheduler history is OFF by default. Enable "Enable All Tasks
  History" in the Actions pane, or the History tab stays empty and there is
  no scheduler-side evidence a run happened. The job's own
  logs/snapshot_job.log is the authoritative record either way.
-->
<Task version="1.2" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>hockey-edge Layer 2 snapshot capture job (--once), every 15 minutes, 11:00-23:00 local.</Description>
    <URI>\hockey-edge snapshot job</URI>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <CalendarTrigger>
      <Repetition>
        <Interval>PT15M</Interval>
        <Duration>PT12H</Duration>
        <!-- false: never hard-kill an in-flight capture at 23:00; let the
             run it's already doing finish. -->
        <StopAtDurationEnd>false</StopAtDurationEnd>
      </Repetition>
      <StartBoundary>2026-09-02T11:00:00</StartBoundary>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
      <ScheduleByDay>
        <DaysInterval>1</DaysInterval>
      </ScheduleByDay>
    </CalendarTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <LogonType>InteractiveToken</LogonType>
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <!-- IgnoreNew: if a run is still going when the next 15-minute tick
         fires, that tick is dropped rather than started alongside it. No two
         instances ever write to snapshots.db at once. -->
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <StartWhenAvailable>true</StartWhenAvailable>
    <RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>false</RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>
    <WakeToRun>true</WakeToRun>
    <!-- PT0S = no execution time limit. Was PT5M, which would hard-kill a
         slow run: a busy night's pass does fixture discovery plus one
         game_detail + game_preview per due game, and a stalled HTTP request
         alone can burn most of five minutes. A killed pass loses that
         window's capture permanently. -->
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT0S</ExecutionTimeLimit>
    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <Hidden>false</Hidden>
    <Priority>7</Priority>
  </Settings>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>C:\Users\Nikla_000\Documents\local-repo\hockey-edge\.venv\Scripts\pythonw.exe</Command>
      <Arguments>-m hockey_edge.snapshot.job --once</Arguments>
      <WorkingDirectory>C:\Users\Nikla_000\Documents\local-repo\hockey-edge</WorkingDirectory>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
